<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Tulun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">835–<a href="/years/884">884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Tulun was a Turkish soldier sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in 868 who made himself its independent ruler and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunid dynasty</a>. He built a strong army and navy, extended his rule into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a>, whose great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a> still stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad ibn Tulun was born on 20 September 835 or slightly later, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. His father, Tulun, was a Turk from a region that Arabic sources call Tagharghar, meaning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> confederation. Tulun was taken captive in 815/6 and sent, with other Turks, as part of the tribute of Nuh ibn Asad, the Samanid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. These slaves became a corps of slave soldiers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, and Tulun rose to command the caliph's private guard. After his death in 854/5, Ahmad came under the care of Yalbakh, a companion of his father, and, according to al-Balawi, the general Bakbak treated him as a son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'tazz gave Bakbak charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Bakbak sent Ahmad as his lieutenant. Ahmad entered Egypt on 27 August 868 and the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, on 15 September. He oversaw the army and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a>, but the land tax lay with the veteran administrator Ibn al-Mudabbir, who had doubled taxes and was widely hated. Ibn Tulun refused the 10,000 dinars that Ibn al-Mudabbir and the postmaster Shukayr offered him on arrival. After four years of intrigue at the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, he had Ibn al-Mudabbir transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in July 871, took over the tax himself and, by 872, controlled every branch of the administration.</p><p>The Abbasid regent al-Muwaffaq was occupied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a>, which left Ibn Tulun room to consolidate his power. In 878, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'tamid, he took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the frontier districts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, though his hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> was uncertain. In 882 his general Lu'lu' defected to al-Muwaffaq and Tarsus refused to acknowledge him. He then convened an assembly of jurists at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, which denounced al-Muwaffaq as a usurper and called for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against him, although neither side moved against the other militarily.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His attempt to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> to heel in the autumn of 883 failed and he fell sick. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and died on 10 May 884, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a> succeeded without opposition. The Tulunid state lasted until January 905, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>; their troops pillaged and razed his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a>, sparing only the great mosque.</p><p>Historians regard his rule as a turning point: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, for the first time since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaohs</a>, ceased to be a passive province and became a political actor in its own right. The realm he built, taking in Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the Jazira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, formed a new political zone, and later Egypt-based regimes, the Ikhshidids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, followed his policies of restoring the economy and keeping an autonomous bureaucracy, army and navy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad ibn Tulun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ahmad ibn Tulun</b>, sent to Egypt in 868, made it a hereditary dominion within a few years.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmad ibn Tulun</b>, a Turkish soldier sent to govern Egypt in 868, made himself its master, and Ya’qub ibn al-Layth, a coppersmith, carved out a kingdom in eastern Iran.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmad ibn Tulun</b>, son of a Turkish slave soldier at the Abbasid court in Samarra, was sent to Egypt as deputy governor in 868.</blockquote><a href="/state/tulunid-emirate">Tulunid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">Al-Qatta'i</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=866" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad ibn Tulun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=866">What was here in 866 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
